---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import PhoneShots from '../components/PhoneShots.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="b-mobile User Guide · b-oss"
  description="How to sign in, browse, post and get notifications with b-mobile, the open-source Blipfoto app for Android."
  nav="b-mobile"
  narrow
>
  <Hero label="Guide hero" title="b-mobile user guide">Browse, post and get notified from your Android phone.</Hero>

  <section class="block" style="border-top: none; padding-top: 0" data-screen-label="Guide body">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <p class="toc-title">Contents</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#get-it">Getting the app</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sign-in">Signing in</a></li>
        <li><a href="#menu">Finding your way around</a></li>
        <li><a href="#browse">Browsing, searching and the map</a></li>
        <li><a href="#entry">Viewing an entry</a></li>
        <li><a href="#post">Posting a new entry</a></li>
        <li><a href="#edit">Editing and deleting your entries</a></li>
        <li><a href="#notifications">Notifications and reminders</a></li>
        <li><a href="#accounts">Using more than one account</a></li>
        <li><a href="#hide-report">Hiding members and reporting</a></li>
        <li><a href="#settings">Settings</a></li>
        <li><a href="#keyboard">Using a keyboard</a></li>
        <li><a href="#limits">What the app can't do</a></li>
        <li><a href="#privacy">Privacy and deleting your data</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="get-it">1. Getting the app</h2>
      <p>
        b-mobile is free, with no ads and no in-app purchases. It will be available from Google
        Play; the <a href="b-mobile">b-mobile page</a> will link to it when it is. It needs
        a phone running Android.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can look around without an account. Sign in to see your own entries and the people you
        follow, and to post, star, favourite and comment.
      </p>

      <h2 id="sign-in">2. Signing in</h2>
      <p>
        Open the menu (the three lines at the top left) and tap <strong>Sign in</strong> at the
        top. To add another account later, go to <strong>Settings → Accounts</strong> and choose
        <strong>Add account</strong>. You sign in on Blipfoto's own sign-in page.
      </p>
      <p>You choose what the app is allowed to do:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Read-write</strong> (the default) lets you post, edit, star, favourite, comment,
          follow and report.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read-only</strong> lets you browse as yourself, including your followed journals,
          but not change anything. Buttons that need write access are hidden or ask you to switch.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The sign-in screen also offers <strong>Get notifications</strong> (see
        <a href="#notifications">Notifications and reminders</a>) and
        <strong>Use browser to sign in</strong>. If your usual browser is already signed in to
        Blipfoto, that is quickest. Turn it off to sign in inside the app instead, which always
        asks for your password.
      </p>

      <h2 id="menu">3. Finding your way around</h2>
      <p>
        Most things are reached from the menu, opened with the button at the top left. The app
        opens on Browse. Tap <strong>b-mobile</strong> in the header at any time to go back to
        Browse.
      </p>
      <PhoneShots
        shots={[{ name: 'menu', alt: 'The b-mobile menu, with your account at the top, then the main screens, and Settings and Help at the bottom.', caption: 'The menu' }]}
      />
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your account</strong> at the top (your picture and username). Tap it to open
          your profile. If you aren't signed in it says <strong>Sign in</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Comments</strong> and <strong>Notifications</strong>, with a count of what's
          unread, and <strong>New entry</strong> to post (read-write accounts only).
        </li>
        <li><strong>Browse</strong>, <strong>Map</strong> and <strong>Search</strong>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Settings</strong> and <strong>Help</strong> side by side at the bottom. The app
          version is shown beneath them.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Screens you reach from the menu show the menu button. Screens you open from inside
        another screen, and Settings and Help, show a back arrow instead. The Android Back button
        goes back one screen too. On Browse it does nothing, so you can't leave the app by
        accident. An open menu closes first.
      </p>

      <h2 id="browse">4. Browsing, searching and the map</h2>
      <PhoneShots
        shots={[{ name: 'browse', alt: 'The Browse screen: a grid of photos with tabs along the top.', caption: 'Browse' }]}
      />
      <p>
        <strong>Browse</strong> has tabs along the top: <strong>Recent</strong>,
        <strong>Following</strong> and <strong>Me</strong> (when you are signed in),
        <strong>Popular</strong>, <strong>Milestones</strong>, <strong>New Blippers</strong> and
        <strong>Nearby</strong>. Swipe the tab bar to see them all. Tap a photo to open it.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Me</strong> shows your own journal, with a calendar and your number of entries.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nearby</strong> needs your location. If you declined permission, use the
          <strong>Allow location</strong> button.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Search</strong> finds entries or people; results update as you type. Tags on an
        entry open a grid of every entry with that tag.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you open an entry from a Browse grid, search results, a tag or someone's Faves, a bar
        at the bottom lets you step through that list with the arrows or by swiping it. Tap its
        caption to see where you came from, hide the bar, or turn it off. The bar is off unless you
        turn it on in <strong>Settings → Appearance</strong>.
      </p>
      <PhoneShots
        shots={[{ name: 'map', alt: 'The map showing pins where blips were taken.', caption: 'Map' }]}
      />
      <p>
        <strong>Map</strong> shows blips that have a location. Pan and zoom, and entries for the
        area you are looking at load as you go. Tap a pin to open a preview of the entry, and use the
        crosshair button to jump to where you are.
      </p>

      <h2 id="entry">5. Viewing an entry</h2>
      <PhoneShots
        shots={[{ name: 'entry', alt: 'An entry: the photo, title, description, view count, star and favourite buttons and the camera details.', caption: 'An entry' }]}
      />
      <p>
        An entry shows the photo, title, description, tags and camera details, and its comments.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Star</strong> and <strong>Favourite</strong> are one-way: once given they can't
          be taken back from the app.
        </li>
        <li>
          Write a <strong>comment</strong>, or <strong>reply</strong> to one. Text you've started
          typing is kept while you look around the app. You can edit or delete a comment where
          Blipfoto allows it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Follow</strong> the author from the author block. Tap a profile picture to see
          it full size.
        </li>
        <li>
          Use the arrows to go to the previous or next entry, the calendar to jump to a date, and
          the history button to see the same day in other years (these need you to be signed in).
        </li>
        <li>
          Tap the expand button, or double-tap the photo, for full screen, where you can zoom and
          pan.
        </li>
        <li>
          On your own entries you can <strong>download</strong> the photo at the size the app
          shows it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you are signed out, tapping star, favourite, comment or follow asks you to sign in
        first, and takes you back to the same place afterwards.
      </p>

      <h2 id="post">6. Posting a new entry</h2>
      <p>
        Choose <strong>New entry</strong> from the menu. You can <strong>take a photo</strong> or
        <strong>choose one</strong> from your gallery. You can also share a photo to b-mobile from
        another app.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Pick the photo. It must be a JPEG or PNG, between 1 KB and 20 MB, with at least one
          side 600 pixels or longer.
        </li>
        <li>
          Add the <strong>details</strong>: a title (up to 50 characters), a description, tags,
          and, if you want, a location on the map. The date starts as the date the photo was
          taken, or today.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tap <strong>Publish</strong> and wait. It is only available when the day is free: Blipfoto
          allows one entry per day, so the app tells you if you already have one for that date,
          or if the date is in the future or too far back.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The photo is sent straight away, so don't leave the app until it finishes. When it has
        gone, you'll see a completion screen with <strong>View entry</strong> and
        <strong>Add another</strong>. If it fails, the entry stays as you left it, with the
        reason, and you can tap <strong>Retry</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you go back with unsaved changes the app asks whether to discard the entry.
      </p>

      <h2 id="edit">7. Editing and deleting your entries</h2>
      <p>
        Open one of your own entries (read-write accounts only) and choose <strong>Edit</strong>.
        You can change the title, description, tags and location, and replace the photo. Tap
        <strong>Save</strong> and wait; don't leave the app until it finishes. If a save fails,
        your changes stay on the screen and you can tap <strong>Retry</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Delete entry</strong> is on the same screen, and asks you to confirm first.
      </p>

      <h2 id="notifications">8. Notifications and reminders</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Notifications</strong> and <strong>Comments</strong> in the menu are your inboxes
        on Blipfoto. They show a badge with the number of unread items. Pull down to refresh.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can also get <strong>phone notifications</strong>, which you choose per account in
        <strong>Settings → Notification settings</strong>: one for new comments and one for new
        notifications. They are optional and off until you turn them on. Android will ask for
        permission, and for a read-write account the app asks you to sign in once more to allow
        it. A notification tells you how many new items there are (for example, "2 new
        comments"), not who they're from. It should arrive within about 5 minutes, and may take
        longer if your phone is saving battery.
        Tapping it opens the right inbox on the right account.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Daily reminder</strong>: in <strong>Settings → Reminders</strong> you can ask for a
        reminder at a time you choose, for each read-write account. It is skipped on days you
        have already posted or edited an entry.
      </p>

      <h2 id="accounts">9. Using more than one account</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Settings → Accounts</strong> lists every account you have signed in with, with its
        mode (read-only or read-write) and whether notifications are on. Tap one to switch to it;
        switching is instant. With two or more accounts, an account button appears at the top of
        the screen for quick switching.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          An account marked red needs you to sign in again. Tap it to do so.
        </li>
        <li>
          Use an account's menu to switch between read-only and read-write, or to
          <strong>Disconnect</strong> it, which signs it out of the app and cancels its
          notifications.
        </li>
        <li>
          By default the app asks which account to use before a star, favourite or comment, when
          you have more than one. You can turn that off in Settings.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="hide-report">10. Hiding members and reporting</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Hide user</strong> removes a member from what you see: their entries show as a
        grey "hidden user" tile, their comments disappear, and they have no pin on the map. The
        list is kept on your phone only, shared by all your accounts, and never sent anywhere.
        Manage it from <strong>Settings → Hidden members</strong>, where you can unhide anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Report</strong> is on entries, comments and profiles. With a read-write account
        you choose at least one reason, add a note if you like, and send it from the app. With a
        read-only or signed-out account the app explains that reporting needs read-write access,
        and can take you to Blipfoto's support form instead. After reporting, it offers to hide the member.
      </p>

      <h2 id="settings">11. Settings</h2>
      <p>
        Open <strong>Settings</strong> from the bottom of the menu. The first group is for the
        app on this phone, and takes effect straight away: <strong>Appearance</strong>,
        <strong>Reminders</strong>, <strong>Accounts</strong> and <strong>Hidden members</strong>.
        The second group is your Blipfoto account settings: <strong>Details &amp; journal</strong>,
        <strong>Profile</strong>, <strong>Notification settings</strong> and, for protected
        journals, <strong>Refused followers</strong>. These need you to be signed in. Most are
        saved with <strong>Save</strong> and <strong>Cancel</strong>, but the
        <strong>New comments</strong> and <strong>New notifications</strong> switches, your
        profile picture and Refused followers apply straight away.
        For what each setting does, see the <a href="settings-reference#b-mobile">settings reference</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="keyboard">12. Using a keyboard</h2>
      <p>If your phone, tablet or computer has a keyboard, these keys work.</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Key</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Tab, Shift+Tab</td>
            <td>Move between buttons, links and photos.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Enter, Space</td>
            <td>Open the highlighted item.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Up, Down</td>
            <td>Scroll the page.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Page Up, Page Down</td>
            <td>Scroll a page at a time.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Home, End</td>
            <td>Jump to the top or the bottom of the page.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Left, Right</td>
            <td>Go to the previous or next page of a photo grid, or the previous or next entry in a journal.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ctrl+Left, Ctrl+Right</td>
            <td>On an entry opened from a list, step along that list, like the arrows on the bar at the bottom.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Backspace</td>
            <td>Go back, when a back arrow is showing. Closes a full-screen photo.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ctrl and +, Ctrl and −</td>
            <td>Zoom a photo grid or a full-screen photo in or out.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ctrl+0</td>
            <td>Reset the zoom.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ctrl+B, Ctrl+I, Ctrl+U, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+L</td>
            <td>When writing a description or comment: bold, italic, underline, strikethrough, link.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="limits">13. What the app can't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Photos are standard size.</strong> Extra images and original-size downloads
          aren't available to apps like b-mobile, so it can't show or save them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Feeds have a limit.</strong> Each feed stops after a fixed number of entries.
          Recent, for example, ends at 900.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Notifications are counts</strong>, not details, and should arrive within about 5
          minutes (longer if your phone is saving battery).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No offline mode.</strong> If a screen can't load, it says whether you seem to be
          offline or Blipfoto can't be reached, and offers a <strong>Try again</strong> button.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="privacy">14. Privacy and deleting your data</h2>
      <p>
        Read the <a href="b-mobile-privacy">b-mobile privacy policy</a> for what the app
        handles and why. To remove your data, see
        <a href="b-mobile-data-deletion">Delete your data</a>. Deleting your Blipfoto
        account itself is done on Blipfoto's website, in your
        <a href="https://www.blipfoto.com/settings/profile">Blipfoto account settings</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Found a problem, or something this guide gets wrong? <a href="contact">Get in touch</a>,
        or open an issue on <a href="https://github.com/IanMStevenson/b-oss/issues">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
